WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.776
One article, and eighteen questions about it.

00:00:02.776 --> 00:00:07.002
Why does a serving engine end up reading that article over and over?

00:00:09.957 --> 00:00:10.972
Hi, I'm Pavlo!

00:00:10.972 --> 00:00:13.678
Flide is an inference engine for decision models.

00:00:13.678 --> 00:00:17.398
Let's see how it shares one state with every question, exactly.

00:00:19.217 --> 00:00:19.906
Chapter one.

00:00:19.906 --> 00:00:21.628
What a decision request costs.

00:00:26.967 --> 00:00:29.152
A decision model never writes a sentence.

00:00:29.152 --> 00:00:33.521
It reads a state, here an article of about five thousand eight hundred tokens.

00:00:33.941 --> 00:00:36.627
Then it scores the options for each question.

00:00:36.627 --> 00:00:38.977
These articles carry about eighteen questions each.

00:00:39.397 --> 00:00:43.330
Run each question as its own prompt, and the article is computed

00:00:43.330 --> 00:00:46.280
every time: over a hundred and seven thousand tokens.

00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:50.342
Compute the state once and branch the questions off it, and

00:00:50.342 --> 00:00:51.666
it's under seventy-five hundred.

00:00:51.666 --> 00:00:52.991
Fourteen times less work.

00:00:55.008 --> 00:00:55.608
Chapter two.

00:00:55.608 --> 00:00:57.408
Why a prefix cache falls short.

00:01:01.192 --> 00:01:03.861
Engines like vLLM already cache a shared prefix.

00:01:03.861 --> 00:01:07.865
For attention that is nearly free: a prefix is just the first

00:01:07.865 --> 00:01:09.200
rows of the cache.

00:01:09.620 --> 00:01:13.418
But most of Qwen three point five's layers are Gated DeltaNet.

00:01:13.418 --> 00:01:15.834
Each folds every token into one matrix.

00:01:15.834 --> 00:01:20.668
You can only resume from a snapshot, and engines take those at block boundaries.

00:01:21.842 --> 00:01:24.208
So each question recomputes a tail.

00:01:24.208 --> 00:01:28.939
On one article, vLLM computes about one point seven times Flide's tokens,

00:01:28.939 --> 00:01:30.910
and SGLang about four times.

00:01:32.508 --> 00:01:33.326
Chapter three.

00:01:33.326 --> 00:01:34.553
One stream, forked.

00:01:39.225 --> 00:01:40.171
Here's the idea.

00:01:40.171 --> 00:01:43.325
Lay every state and question out as one flat stream.

00:01:43.325 --> 00:01:47.740
Then fork the state, so each question sees only its own state and itself.

00:01:49.217 --> 00:01:51.345
The paper draws a request as a tree.

00:01:51.345 --> 00:01:54.272
The state is the trunk, and each question is a branch.

00:01:54.692 --> 00:01:58.295
On the GPU, the tree becomes one flat stream: all the states

00:01:58.295 --> 00:01:59.797
first, then all the questions.

00:02:01.492 --> 00:02:03.176
Here is the article's example.

00:02:03.176 --> 00:02:07.216
Six state tokens, then three questions of three, two and four tokens,

00:02:07.216 --> 00:02:08.900
all in one flat stream.

00:02:09.320 --> 00:02:14.031
The state rows are ordinary causal attention: each token reads the ones before it.

00:02:14.451 --> 00:02:18.609
Each question row reads the whole state, then its own branch up to itself.

00:02:18.609 --> 00:02:19.796
Its siblings stay dark.

00:02:20.216 --> 00:02:24.034
Set one question's rows beside the same question asked alone.

00:02:24.034 --> 00:02:25.944
Cell for cell, they match.

00:02:25.944 --> 00:02:27.853
Fifteen tokens instead of twenty-seven.

00:02:29.592 --> 00:02:32.760
A mask can't separate questions in a recurrent layer.

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:37.335
If question two follows question one, it inherits question one through the state.

00:02:37.755 --> 00:02:40.096
So Flide runs each such layer twice.

00:02:40.096 --> 00:02:43.774
Call one reads every state and keeps each request's final snapshot.

00:02:44.194 --> 00:02:49.182
Call two runs every question at once, each restarting from its own request's snapshot.

00:02:49.182 --> 00:02:53.100
In the decoder that is one argument: an initial state per

00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:54.525
branch, picked by index.

00:02:54.525 --> 00:02:55.594
Nothing is copied.

00:02:56.014 --> 00:02:59.450
The head reads each question's last token and scores its options.

00:03:01.092 --> 00:03:04.274
The article checked this on a small NumPy hybrid.

00:03:04.274 --> 00:03:07.809
The fork matched every answer asked alone bit for bit.

00:03:07.809 --> 00:03:10.991
Masking alone, or a delimiter per question, flipped answers.

00:03:10.991 --> 00:03:13.112
Neither half works without the other.

00:03:14.592 --> 00:03:15.219
Chapter four.

00:03:15.219 --> 00:03:17.415
Where nine point five times comes from.

00:03:22.092 --> 00:03:24.054
The launch animation tells it.

00:03:24.054 --> 00:03:27.586
One article becomes eighteen prompts, each repeating the article.

00:03:28.006 --> 00:03:31.710
Flide computes it once and forks it eighteen ways.

00:03:31.710 --> 00:03:37.473
Thirty-seven point seven milliseconds, against about three hundred and sixty for vLLM and SGLang.

00:03:39.175 --> 00:03:41.799
But that is one cell: the smallest model.

00:03:41.799 --> 00:03:44.423
Move along the row and the lead shrinks.

00:03:44.423 --> 00:03:48.687
At twenty-seven billion it is one point five, close to the token ratio.

00:03:48.687 --> 00:03:52.296
At small sizes, most of the gap is per-question front-end work.

00:03:53.842 --> 00:03:57.348
Where forking really pays is many questions per state.

00:03:57.348 --> 00:04:01.633
As questions grow, the baselines climb roughly linearly, and Flide adds

00:04:01.633 --> 00:04:03.191
only each branch's tokens.

00:04:03.191 --> 00:04:07.086
At sixty-four questions it is twelve point seven times faster.

00:04:07.506 --> 00:04:11.353
And for an agent whose history grows, Flide checkpoints the state and

00:04:11.353 --> 00:04:12.956
computes only the new tokens.

00:04:14.442 --> 00:04:16.244
Exact has a precise meaning here.

00:04:17.031 --> 00:04:21.141
It means each answer equals the question asked alone, at the same precision.

00:04:21.561 --> 00:04:24.489
It does not mean equal to a float32 pass.

00:04:24.489 --> 00:04:28.392
In sixteen-bit floats, Flide still flips up to two point three percent.

00:04:28.812 --> 00:04:31.607
And by default, bits move with server load.

00:04:31.607 --> 00:04:34.402
A batch-invariant flag pins them, at some latency.

00:04:35.767 --> 00:04:38.532
Keep the tree as metadata and run one stream.

00:04:38.532 --> 00:04:41.912
The state is computed once, and every answer stays its own.

00:04:44.442 --> 00:04:45.174
Mask attention.

00:04:45.174 --> 00:04:46.639
Fork the recurrent state.

00:04:46.639 --> 00:04:50.666
And expect the biggest gains on small models with many questions.

00:04:50.666 --> 00:04:51.765
Thanks for watching!

00:04:52.185 --> 00:04:54.159
Every source is in the full article.

00:04:54.159 --> 00:04:54.723
I'm Pavlo.

00:04:54.723 --> 00:04:55.005
Bye!
